# The normal kernel with a network device (`--net`): `init` starts `ethd` and
# `netd`, gives the shell its root network connection, and `net status` prints
# the lease the service holds (ADR-0221 item 4, ADR-0229 item 4, N2). Under
# the test runner's `restrict=on` the lease has no router and no DNS server.
! panicked
! first process ended
< ethd: holds one channel, to the network service
< netd: holds no device, interrupt or pin
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n/>
# The lease takes a few hundred milliseconds under TCG.
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n/>
> net status\r
< net0  up  52:54:00:12:34:56
< address   10.0.2.15/24 (dhcp, lease
< frames
< \r\n/>
# `net` alone is the same; a word that is not `status` is a usage mistake.
> net\r
< net0  up  52:54:00:12:34:56
< \r\n/>
> net lease\r
< net: lease: net shows the state: net status
< \r\n/>
> net --help\r
< net: show the state of the network
< \r\n/>
x power off
> poweroff\r
